From: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Subject: proc: call security_task_to_inode() under i_lock
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 22:03:02 -0800

A task which has just changed its LSM label can be refused access to its
own /proc/<pid> files when another process looks up the same files at
the same time.

To demonstrate, with Smack: a task without CAP_MAC_OVERRIDE alternately
writes "AAA" and "BBB" to /proc/self/attr/current and after each write
opens its own /proc/<pid>/environ.  Two other processes stat()
/proc/<pid>, /proc/<pid>/environ and /proc/<pid>/attr/current.  Under QEMU
the open() failed with -EACCES once in 84,256 attempts.  With a 300
microsecond delay added to smack_task_to_inode() between reading the
task's label and storing it, the open() failed 299 times in 22,372
attempts and the write to attr/current failed 318 times.  With this patch
neither failed, with the delay (12,898 attempts) or without (22,168).

This is the same problem as the one fixed for the inode's owner by "proc:
serialize task owner lookup and inode updates".  security_task_to_inode()
is called after i_lock has been dropped.  smack_task_to_inode() and
selinux_task_to_inode() read the task's label and then store it in the
inode's security blob.  Smack does this with no lock and SELinux reads the
label before it takes isec->lock.  So two lookups of one entry can store
in either order, and a label which was read before the task changed it can
overwrite the one which was read afterwards.  The inode then has a stale
label until the next lookup.  A check which uses the stale label can
wrongly refuse access, as above, and for the same reason could wrongly
allow it.

Call security_task_to_inode() before dropping i_lock, so that the LSM's
read of the task and its update of the inode are serialized along with the
owner update.

The hook could not sleep before this change either: pid_revalidate() calls
it under rcu_read_lock().  SELinux takes isec->lock inside it, which now
nests inside i_lock.  Nothing in security/ takes i_lock.

