NOTES (everything from here to the diffstat is not changelog).

- Goes on top of proc-serialize-task-owner-updates.patch.
- No Signed-off-by, Fixes: or stable Cc from me.
- SELinux: I did not demonstrate a stale label there (it needs a policy
  with a domain transition).  The claim for SELinux is from reading
  selinux_task_to_inode(): the SID is read before isec->lock is taken.
- The "wrongly allow" direction is by the same reasoning and I did not
  try to produce it.  The LSM people should judge how much that matters;
  the contents of the sensitive /proc/<pid> files are also guarded by
  ptrace-style checks against the task itself, which do not use the
  inode's label.
- The alternative is to fix each LSM (read the label under isec->lock
  in SELinux; add locking to Smack).  This way covers all of them in
  one place, including any BPF LSM program on this hook, at the price of
  calling the hook with i_lock held.
- "Nothing in security/ takes i_lock" is from grep over security/selinux,
  security/smack, security/apparmor and security/security.c.

Tested under QEMU, 2 CPUs, kernel with SELinux, Smack and AppArmor built
in, PROVE_LOCKING and DEBUG_ATOMIC_SLEEP:
- the Smack demonstration above (lsm=smack), with and without this
  patch, with and without the test-only delay;
- lsm=selinux (no policy loaded) and lsm=smack: three processes looking
  up /proc/<pid>, status, fd/*, fdinfo/*, ns/pid, map_files/*, task/*
  while the target flips its euid and is ptrace-seized: no warnings.
Not tested: SELinux with a policy, AppArmor active, BPF LSM, PREEMPT_RT
with this patch (the first patch was tested on RT).

FOR THE TESTING CHAT: boot with lsm=smack (no Smack rules needed).
Target, as root: drop CAP_MAC_OVERRIDE from the effective and permitted
sets, then loop { write "AAA" to /proc/self/attr/current; open
/proc/<own pid>/environ and count EACCES; the same with "BBB" }.  Two
observers with full capabilities: loop stat() on the target's
/proc/<pid>, /proc/<pid>/environ and /proc/<pid>/attr/current.  Expect
about one EACCES per 100,000 opens without the patch and none with it.
With SELinux in enforcing mode and a policy: run the selinux-testsuite,
and something which does setcon()/exec transitions while "ps -eZ" loops.

 fs/proc/base.c |    4 ++--
 fs/proc/fd.c   |    2 +-
 2 files changed, 3 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/fs/proc/base.c~proc-call-security_task_to_inode-under-i_lock
+++ a/fs/proc/base.c
@@ -2033,8 +2033,8 @@ void pid_update_inode(struct task_struct
 	task_dump_owner(task, inode->i_mode, &inode->i_uid, &inode->i_gid);
 
 	inode->i_mode &= ~(S_ISUID | S_ISGID);
-	spin_unlock(&inode->i_lock);
 	security_task_to_inode(task, inode);
+	spin_unlock(&inode->i_lock);
 }
 
 /*
@@ -2214,9 +2214,9 @@ static int map_files_d_revalidate(struct
 	if (exact_vma_exists) {
 		spin_lock(&inode->i_lock);
 		task_dump_owner(task, 0, &inode->i_uid, &inode->i_gid);
+		security_task_to_inode(task, inode);
 		spin_unlock(&inode->i_lock);
 
-		security_task_to_inode(task, inode);
 		status = 1;
 	}
 
--- a/fs/proc/fd.c~proc-call-security_task_to_inode-under-i_lock
+++ a/fs/proc/fd.c
@@ -138,8 +138,8 @@ static void tid_fd_update_inode(struct t
 			i_mode |= S_IWUSR | S_IXUSR;
 		inode->i_mode = i_mode;
 	}
-	spin_unlock(&inode->i_lock);
 	security_task_to_inode(task, inode);
+	spin_unlock(&inode->i_lock);
 }
 
 static int tid_fd_revalidate(struct inode *dir, const struct qstr *name,
_
